#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166511 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166950 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7cc1e18-810d-54cd-90d7-354a865105a7" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Expression for the LTE for a Given Numerical Method"}] :question/id #uuid "db842cc3-76cc-5a6c-b293-3c5f21c58002" :question/math-academy-id "e-21214" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to approximate the solution to an initial value problem using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf the exact solution is $y_{\\textrm{exact}}(x) = x^3,$ find an expression for the local truncation error for the step from $x$ to $x_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x.$" :question/worked-solution "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a *single step* of a numerical method, when the step begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value $y_{\\textrm{exact}}(x)$ to $x_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact solution of the initial value problem is $y_{\\textrm{exact}}(x) = x^3.$ Now, let's find the $y$-increment given by the numerical method.\n\nFirst, the slope at a step starting at $x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(x)=({x}^{3})^{′}=3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the increment given by the numerical method is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(x)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at $x_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(x+Δx) \\\\  & =(x+Δx)^{3} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}\\cdot Δx+3x\\cdot (Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for step $n$ starting at $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy) \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}\\cdot Δx+3x\\cdot (Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{3}]-[{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}\\cdot Δx] \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-{x}^{3}+(3{x}^{2}-3{x}^{2})\\cdot Δx+3x\\cdot (Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{3} \\\\  & =3x\\cdot (Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166512 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166951 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c8905c41-cedb-5c7b-81f7-7639c5451174" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Global Truncation Error Given Numerical Solutions to an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "b0f9cdf5-ca84-537a-9b3e-46b7c56729f1" :question/math-academy-id "e-21218" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate a solution to an initial value problem, resulting in the table below. If the exact solution is $y(x) = \\sqrt{x + 2},$ find, rounded to three decimal places, the global truncation error at each step.\n\n| $n$ | $x_n$ | $y_n$ | $e_n$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.414$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.3$ | $1.480$ |  |\n| $2$ | $0.6$ | $1.570$ |  |\n| $3$ | $0.9$ | $1.650$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "The global truncation error (GTE) is the total error accumulated after several steps of a numerical method. The GTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x_{n-1}$ to $x_n = x_{n-1} + \\Delta x$ is\n\n$$\ne_n = y(x_n) - y_n,\n$$\n\nwhere $y(x_n)$ is the exact value at $x_n,$ and $y_n$ is the numerical value produced after $n$ steps.\n\nWe are given the numerical values $y_n$ produced at each step in the table. Now, we compute the exact value $y(x_n)$ at each value of $x_n{:}$\n\n- The exact value at $x_0=0$ is $y(0) = \\sqrt{0 + 2} = \\sqrt{2}.$\n\n- The exact value at $x_1=0.3$ is $y(0.3) = \\sqrt{0.3 + 2}.$\n\n- The exact value at $x_2=0.6$ is $y(0.6) = \\sqrt{0.6 + 2}.$\n\n- The exact value at $x_3=0.9$ is $y(0.9) = \\sqrt{0.9 + 2}.$\n\nLet's add these values to the table.\n\n| $n$ | $x_n$ | $y_n$ | $y(x_n)$ | $e_n$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.414$ | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.3$ | $1.480$ | $\\sqrt{2.3}$ |  |\n| $2$ | $0.6$ | $1.570$ | $\\sqrt{2.6}$ |  |\n| $3$ | $0.9$ | $1.650$ | $\\sqrt{2.9}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we find the GTE at each step, rounding to three decimal places in each case:\n\n- The GTE of the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{1} & =y({x}_{1})-{y}_{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2.3}-1.480 \\\\  & ≈0.037.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The GTE of the second step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{2} & =y({x}_{2})-{y}_{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2.6}-1.570 \\\\  & ≈0.042.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The GTE of the third step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{3} & =y({x}_{3})-{y}_{3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2.9}-1.650 \\\\  & ≈0.053.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $n$ | $x_n$ | $y_n$ | $y(x_n)$ | $e_n$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.414$ | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.3$ | $1.480$ | $\\sqrt{2.3}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{0.037}$ |\n| $2$ | $0.6$ | $1.570$ | $\\sqrt{2.6}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{0.042}$ |\n| $3$ | $0.9$ | $1.650$ | $\\sqrt{2.9}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{0.053}$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166538 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166949 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77130223-3730-5a35-bd28-d457f079dad3" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the LTE for a Numerical Method Given an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "2d820110-30f7-5fd8-a30c-e21d45cc789f" :question/math-academy-id "e-21604" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = -\\sin x, \\qquad y(0) = 1\n$$\n\nSuppose we wish to approximate the solution using the following method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the exact solution is $y_{\\textrm{exact}}(x) = \\cos x,$ compute the local truncation error $\\tau_{6}$ for the step starting at $x=1$ with step size $\\Delta x=0.2.$ Round your answer to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a *single step* of a numerical method, when the step begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value $y_{\\textrm{exact}}(x)$ to $x_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact solution is $y_{\\textrm{exact}}(x) = \\cos x.$ So, the $y$-value at the start of the step $x=1$ is $y_{\\textrm{exact}}(1)=\\cos(1).$\n\nNow, let's find the $y$-increment given by the numerical method.\n\nFirst, the slope at the start of the step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(1) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1) \\\\  & =-0.8415.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the increment at this step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(1)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =-0.8415\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & ≈-0.1683.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at $x_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(x+Δx) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(1+0.2) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(1.2) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for the step starting at $x=1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{6} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.2)-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)+Δy) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.2)-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)-0.1683) \\\\  & ≈-0.010\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166539 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166948 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9477a5ac-7e05-5fa4-9cf3-352cc17f2e1b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Local Truncation Error Given Numerical Solutions to an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "099fbdba-a319-5a75-af75-b2bbdb1b3494" :question/math-academy-id "e-21605" :question/problem "An initial value problem has the exact solution $y_{\\textrm{exact}}(x) = \\cos(2x).$ A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to the initial value problem, resulting in the table below, where $y$ denotes the approximate solution at each step, and $\\tau_n$ is the local truncation error at each step. At each step, the exact value is used to approximate the new value. \n\nCalculate the local truncation error at each step. Round your answers to three decimal places.\n\n| $n$ | $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.000$ |\n| $1$ | $0.2$ | $0.925$ |\n| $2$ | $0.4$ | $0.692$ |\n| $3$ | $0.6$ | $0.368$ |" :question/worked-solution "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a *single step* of a numerical method, when the step begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value $y_{\\textrm{exact}}(x)$ to $x_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the LTE at each step, rounding to three decimal places in each case:\n\n- After the first step, we have $y_\\text{new} = 0.925.$ So, the LTE of the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{1} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.2)-0.925 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.4)-0.925 \\\\  & ≈-0.004.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- After the second step, we have $y_\\text{new} = 0.692.$ So, the LTE of the second step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{2} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.4)-0.692 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.8)-0.692 \\\\  & ≈0.005.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- After the third step, we have $y_\\text{new} = 0.368.$ So, the LTE of the third step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{3} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.6)-0.368 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.2)-0.368 \\\\  & ≈-0.006.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $n$ | $x$ | $y$ | $\\tau_n$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.000$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.2$ | $0.925$ | $-0.004$ |\n| $2$ | $0.4$ | $0.692$ | $0.005$ |\n| $3$ | $0.6$ | $0.368$ | $-0.006$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308516 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166950 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7cc1e18-810d-54cd-90d7-354a865105a7" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Expression for the LTE for a Given Numerical Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01ce88f1-c43c-5694-86f2-e5f123d221db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{x}^{4}+33{x}^{3}+30{x}^{2}+6x+5\\cdot Δx+3(Δx)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305167} {:answer/id #uuid "1680297a-1b0f-5210-9d16-9b70ab90b62d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x-{x}^{3})\\cdot Δx+2(Δx)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305352} {:answer/id #uuid "432d495f-81bc-55c2-93a8-b16231491b26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x-2{x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\cdot Δx+3(Δx)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305815} {:answer/id #uuid "c3252cfe-9c90-5d54-8748-1edbe78c7c2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-9{x}^{4}-33{x}^{3}-30{x}^{2}+6x+5)\\cdot Δx+3(Δx)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306970} {:answer/id #uuid "c48881e3-2349-56d1-a602-7efe0995f387" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-{x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306982}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3252cfe-9c90-5d54-8748-1edbe78c7c2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-9{x}^{4}-33{x}^{3}-30{x}^{2}+6x+5)\\cdot Δx+3(Δx)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306970} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae893c59-7e49-5b0c-a55c-55a5f74e450d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308077}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "726f6f46-0815-5100-8694-30312d46b17e" :question/math-academy-id "q-333837" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to approximate the solution to an initial value problem using the following numerical method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =(xy+{y}^{2})\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)=3{x}^{2}+5x,$ find an expression for the local truncation error for the step from $x$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx.$" :question/worked-solution "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a single step of a numerical method, when the step begins from an exact value. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact solution of the initial value problem is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)=3{x}^{2}+5x.$ Now, let's find the $y$-increment given by the numerical method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =(x\\cdot {y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+{y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)^{2})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(x\\cdot (3{x}^{2}+5x)+(3{x}^{2}+5x)^{2})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(3{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+9{x}^{4}+30{x}^{3}+25{x}^{2})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(9{x}^{4}+33{x}^{3}+30{x}^{2})\\cdot Δx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at ${x}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & =({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δx) \\\\  & =3(x+Δx)^{2}+5(x+Δx) \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}+2x\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2})+5x+5(Δx) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+6x\\cdot Δx+3(Δx)^{2}+5x+5(Δx) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+5x+(6x+5)\\cdot Δx+3(Δx)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for step $n$ starting at $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & =[3{x}^{2}+5x+(6x+5)\\cdot Δx+3(Δx)^{2}]-[3{x}^{2}+5x+(9{x}^{4}+33{x}^{3}+30{x}^{2})\\cdot Δx] \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}-3{x}^{2}+5x-5x+(6x+5)\\cdot Δx+3(Δx)^{2}-(9{x}^{4}+33{x}^{3}+30{x}^{2})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(6x+5)\\cdot Δx+3(Δx)^{2}-(9{x}^{4}+33{x}^{3}+30{x}^{2})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-9{x}^{4}-33{x}^{3}-30{x}^{2}+6x+5)\\cdot Δx+3(Δx)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308517 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166950 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7cc1e18-810d-54cd-90d7-354a865105a7" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Expression for the LTE for a Given Numerical Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16fc9a3d-3c68-579e-bfba-e7578c0e600a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+x+1))\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305362} {:answer/id #uuid "50e2a83d-cb8f-560f-afdc-86867e851747" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+x+1))\\cdot Δx+2(Δx)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305936} {:answer/id #uuid "51cbdede-36da-552a-866d-2695fa7eb3fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1))\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305947} {:answer/id #uuid "93dc4d22-a59d-5f10-b807-b8ba30a6b355" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+x+1))\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306547} {:answer/id #uuid "ba62fc43-a7a0-59e1-9f38-1878783b4aa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{x}^{2}))\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93dc4d22-a59d-5f10-b807-b8ba30a6b355" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+x+1))\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306547} :answer-field/id #uuid "688840c8-5b5e-5144-a5f5-777b15c7c073" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "baf3125d-0d25-502b-857d-9bbe768e2a62" :question/math-academy-id "q-333838" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to approximate the solution to an initial value problem using the following numerical method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)={x}^{2}+1,$ find an expression for the local truncation error for the step from $x$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx.$" :question/worked-solution "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a single step of a numerical method, when the step begins from an exact value. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact solution of the initial value problem is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)={x}^{2}+1.$ Now, let's find the $y$-increment given by the numerical method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{y}_{\\text{exact}}(x))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{x}^{2}+1)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+x+1)\\cdot Δx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at ${x}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & =({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δx) \\\\  & =(x+Δx)^{2}+1 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+2x\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for step $n$ starting at $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}+2x\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2}+1]-[{x}^{2}+1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+x+1)\\cdot Δx] \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-{x}^{2}+1-1+(2x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+x+1))\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2} \\\\  & =(2x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+x+1))\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308631 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166951 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c8905c41-cedb-5c7b-81f7-7639c5451174" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Global Truncation Error Given Numerical Solutions to an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09270b12-1e06-5195-bf62-c94288501256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.040" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305236}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09270b12-1e06-5195-bf62-c94288501256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.040" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305236} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e5d3453-4541-5e28-a765-1b6be8713e4b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307620} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c71ca72d-684b-56a0-87fa-6b5461483469" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.105" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307005}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c71ca72d-684b-56a0-87fa-6b5461483469" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.105" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307005} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e5be080-86e2-5313-96fd-84fb724466d5" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307715} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89d0b50e-e98a-549b-aff3-fda0ab70f37e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.010" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306466}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89d0b50e-e98a-549b-aff3-fda0ab70f37e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.010" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306466} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b1aebd2-e893-5197-a788-c4691c654614" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307908}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c8e645d5-fc08-5104-ac6e-e5cda6447fc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-334486" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate a solution to an initial value problem, resulting in the table below. If the exact solution is $y(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+1,$ find, rounded to three decimal places, the global truncation error at each step.\n\n| $n$ | ${x}_{n}$ | ${y}_{n}$ | ${e}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.2$ | $1.010$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $2$ | $0.4$ | $1.040$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $3$ | $0.7$ | $1.140$ | {{field-3}} |" :question/worked-solution "The global truncation error (GTE) is the total error accumulated after several steps of a numerical method. The GTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from ${x}_{n-1}$ to ${x}_{n}={x}_{n-1}+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n{e}_{n}=y({x}_{n})-{y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $y({x}_{n})$ is the exact value at ${x}_{n},$ and ${y}_{n}$ is the numerical value produced after $n$ steps.\n\nWe are given the numerical values ${y}_{n}$ produced at each step in the table. Now, we compute the exact value $y({x}_{n})$ at each value of ${x}_{n}:$\n\n- The exact value at ${x}_{0}=0$ is $y(0)=\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}+1=1.$\n\n- The exact value at ${x}_{1}=0.2$ is $y(0.2)=\\frac{1}{2}(0.2)^{2}+1.$\n\n- The exact value at ${x}_{2}=0.4$ is $y(0.4)=\\frac{1}{2}(0.4)^{2}+1.$\n\n- The exact value at ${x}_{3}=0.7$ is $y(0.7)=\\frac{1}{2}(0.7)^{2}+1.$\n\nLet's add these values to the table.\n\n| $n$ | ${x}_{n}$ | ${y}_{n}$ | $y({x}_{n})$ | ${e}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.2$ | $1.010$ | $\\frac{1}{2}(0.2)^{2}+1$ |  |\n| $2$ | $0.4$ | $1.040$ | $\\frac{1}{2}(0.4)^{2}+1$ |  |\n| $3$ | $0.7$ | $1.140$ | $\\frac{1}{2}(0.7)^{2}+1$ |  |\n\nFinally, we find the GTE at each step, rounding to three decimal places in each case:\n\n- The GTE of the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{1} & =y({x}_{1})-{y}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(0.2)^{2}+1-1.010 \\\\  & =0.010.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The GTE of the second step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{2} & =y({x}_{2})-{y}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(0.4)^{2}+1-1.040 \\\\  & =0.040.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The GTE of the third step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{3} & =y({x}_{3})-{y}_{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(0.7)^{2}+1-1.140 \\\\  & =0.105.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $n$ | ${x}_{n}$ | ${y}_{n}$ | $y({x}_{n})$ | ${e}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.2$ | $1.010$ | $\\frac{1}{2}(0.2)^{2}+1$ | $0.010$ |\n| $2$ | $0.4$ | $1.040$ | $\\frac{1}{2}(0.4)^{2}+1$ | $0.040$ |\n| $3$ | $0.7$ | $1.140$ | $\\frac{1}{2}(0.7)^{2}+1$ | $0.105$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308632 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166951 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c8905c41-cedb-5c7b-81f7-7639c5451174" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Global Truncation Error Given Numerical Solutions to an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1dbb0d8-3902-52b2-b225-76ce2a8ffd69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.001" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307379}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1dbb0d8-3902-52b2-b225-76ce2a8ffd69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.001" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307379} :answer-field/id #uuid "04a07384-2136-552c-8032-6914cc10aaea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307536} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4682b599-1a32-57ce-9745-4b906274f0fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.018" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4682b599-1a32-57ce-9745-4b906274f0fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.018" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305839} :answer-field/id #uuid "2badc607-d015-53d4-b776-b474e763763a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307656} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83fd4bfd-5f29-5a67-8b12-9184670e8ef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.020" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83fd4bfd-5f29-5a67-8b12-9184670e8ef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.020" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306409} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb5567f5-30d6-5f84-bad2-a6f285e3a4e6" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "967bfe51-8535-5865-8de7-5112e7c8da0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-334489" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate a solution to an initial value problem, resulting in the table below. If the exact solution is $y(x)=\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}},$ find, rounded to three decimal places, the global truncation error at each step.\n\n| $n$ | ${x}_{n}$ | ${y}_{n}$ | ${e}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.25$ | $1.030$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $2$ | $0.5$ | $1.100$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $3$ | $0.75$ | $1.230$ | {{field-3}} |" :question/worked-solution "The global truncation error (GTE) is the total error accumulated after several steps of a numerical method. The GTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from ${x}_{n-1}$ to ${x}_{n}={x}_{n-1}+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n{e}_{n}=y({x}_{n})-{y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $y({x}_{n})$ is the exact value at ${x}_{n},$ and ${y}_{n}$ is the numerical value produced after $n$ steps.\n\nWe are given the numerical values ${y}_{n}$ produced at each step in the table. Now, we compute the exact value $y({x}_{n})$ at each value of ${x}_{n}:$\n\n- The exact value at ${x}_{0}=0$ is $y(0)=\\sqrt{1+{0}^{2}}=1.$\n\n- The exact value at ${x}_{1}=0.25$ is $y(0.25)=\\sqrt{1+(0.25)^{2}}.$\n\n- The exact value at ${x}_{2}=0.5$ is $y(0.5)=\\sqrt{1+(0.5)^{2}}.$\n\n- The exact value at ${x}_{3}=0.75$ is $y(0.75)=\\sqrt{1+(0.75)^{2}}.$\n\nLet's add these values to the table.\n\n| $n$ | ${x}_{n}$ | ${y}_{n}$ | $y({x}_{n})$ | ${e}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.25$ | $1.030$ | $\\sqrt{1+(0.25)^{2}}$ |  |\n| $2$ | $0.5$ | $1.100$ | $\\sqrt{1+(0.5)^{2}}$ |  |\n| $3$ | $0.75$ | $1.230$ | $\\sqrt{1+(0.75)^{2}}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we find the GTE at each step, rounding to three decimal places in each case:\n\n- The GTE of the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{1} & =y({x}_{1})-{y}_{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+(0.25)^{2}}-1.030 \\\\  & ≈0.001.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The GTE of the second step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{2} & =y({x}_{2})-{y}_{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+(0.5)^{2}}-1.100 \\\\  & ≈0.018.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The GTE of the third step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{3} & =y({x}_{3})-{y}_{3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+(0.75)^{2}}-1.230 \\\\  & =0.020.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $n$ | ${x}_{n}$ | ${y}_{n}$ | $y({x}_{n})$ | ${e}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.25$ | $1.030$ | $\\sqrt{1+(0.25)^{2}}$ | $0.001$ |\n| $2$ | $0.5$ | $1.100$ | $\\sqrt{1+(0.5)^{2}}$ | $0.018$ |\n| $3$ | $0.75$ | $1.230$ | $\\sqrt{1+(0.75)^{2}}$ | $0.020$ |"}]}
